# Keep build contexts small. Docker sends the whole context to the daemon before
# a single instruction runs, and this tree can carry gigabytes of build cache and
# release artifacts: an unfiltered `docker build .` here once shipped 17GB.
#
# The website image also narrows its COPY to the paths it actually compiles, which
# is the stronger guarantee - a missing rule here cannot silently re-inflate it.
# This file is the second line of defence, and it covers any future image built
# from this repo.
#
# NOTE: ssd deploys are unaffected either way. It ships the context with
# `git archive --format=tar HEAD`, so untracked files never reach a server build.
# This matters for LOCAL builds, where untracked caches are present and huge.

# Build caches and outputs
bit-out/
dist/out/

# EXCEPTION: docker/toolchain.Dockerfile builds the published toolchain image FROM
# the release tarballs, so it needs them in its context. Excluding dist/out/
# outright broke that build with `lstat /dist/out: no such file or directory` - and
# because the failure was piped through grep, a stale local tag got pushed instead.
# Later rules win in .dockerignore, so this re-admits only the artifacts.
!dist/out/*.tar.xz

# Version control
.git/
.gitignore

# Machine-local deploy config (gitignored anyway; never belongs in an image)
.ssd/

# Editor and OS noise
.DS_Store
*.swp
